==Currency== <!--T:28-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|CUR|India}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:29-->
{{OK}} The Indian government holds the copyright of the images of most denominations of Indian currency, including the ₹1 note, but reproduction is permitted. Please use {{tl|GODL-India}}.

==Freedom of panorama== <!--T:30-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|FOP|India}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:31-->
*{{OkMark}}. For 3D (architecture and sculptures) {{tl|FoP-India}},
*{{NotMark}}. For copyrighted 2D (paintings, drawings, maps, pictures, engravings, etc.)
Under the ''Copyright Act, 1957 (Act No. 14 of 1957, as amended up to Act No. 27 of 2012)'', the following acts shall not constitute an infringement of copyright:{{sref|1957-2012 Section 52}}
</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:32-->
*The making or publishing of a painting, drawing, engraving or photograph of a work of architecture or the display of a work of architecture;{{sref|1957-2012 Section 52(s)}}
*The making or publishing of a painting, drawing, engraving or photograph of a sculpture, or other artistic work falling under sub-clause (iii) of clause (c) of section 2, if such work is permanently situate in a public place or any premises to which the public has access;{{sref|1957-2012 Section 52(t)}}
*:Section 2(c)(iii) reads "any other work of artistic craftsmanship;".{{sref|1957-2012 Section 2(c)(iii)}} Paintings, drawings, or photographs fall under Section 2(c){i).{{sref|1957-2012 Section 2(c)(i)}}
*The inclusion in a cinematograph film of (i) any artistic work permanently situate in a public place or any premises to which the public has access;{{sref|1957-2012 Section 52(u)}}
</translate>

;<translate><!--T:43--> Case/s</translate>
*<translate><!--T:44--> [<tvar name="1">https://indiankanoon.org/doc/1613396/</tvar> ''The Daily Calendar Supplying ... vs The United Concern on 16 January, 1964''], by J. Ramakrishnan (by Indian Kanoon)</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:33-->
Indian law is modelled on UK law, and in the scarcity of more specific case laws to the contrary it is reasonable to assume that the rules will be similar. See {{pg|Commons:Copyright rules by territory/United Kingdom|anchor=FOP|COM:FOP United Kingdom}} for more details.

==Stamps== <!--T:34-->

</translate><noinclude>{{comseealso|Commons:Stamps/Public domain}}
</noinclude>
<translate>
<!--T:35-->
[[File:Red copyright.svg|25px|link=|Copyrighted]] As laid out in [[<tvar name="1">:File:PO ORDER Conditions Related to Reproduction of Stamps page 1.jpg</tvar>|this official document]] the reuse of stamps is regulated by multiple terms that aren't compatible with Commons. So stamps are copyrighted per the normal term for government works of 60+ years after the date of publication. [[<tvar name="2">:en:Wikipedia talk:Notice board for India-related topics/Archive 25#Indian_Stamps</tvar>|here]] and [[<tvar name="3">:en:Wikipedia:Administrators' noticeboard/Archive59#Fair use stamps: revisitied ...</tvar>|here]] for related discussions.

<!--T:36-->
Use either {{t|GODL-India}} or {{t|PD-India}} where appropriate. 
Material issued by the Government of India before independence may be covered by {{t|PD-UKGov}}.

==Threshold of originality== <!--T:37-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|TOO|India}}
</noinclude><translate>
<!--T:38-->
India seems to have a similar threshold of originality as the US Courts, called ''Modicum of Creativity''. Older cases may have similar thresholds of originality to the UK Courts called {{wp-Sweat of the brow|Sweat of the brow}} but this is no longer applied. 
Robbin Singh has written an essay on the subject that may be useful.</translate><ref name=Singh/>
